<Project>

  <!--
    NuGet vulnerability gate (specs/005-handle-nuget-packages-vulnerabilities).

    Every restore, local or CI, audits every package this solution resolves,
    including transitive ones, against the GitHub Advisory Database via
    nuget.org. Any known advisory of any severity fails the restore:

      NU1901 low · NU1902 moderate · NU1903 high · NU1904 critical

    NU1900 (advisory feed unreachable) deliberately stays a warning. It is
    visible in the output but doesn't make builds depend on the feed's uptime
    (research.md Decision 5).

    To fix a finding: upgrade the *parent* package that pulls it in first. Use a
    direct PackageReference pin only as a fallback, with a comment linking
    the advisory and stating when to remove it (research.md Decisions 1 and 6).
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
    <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
    <NuGetAuditLevel>low</NuGetAuditLevel>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1901;NU1902;NU1903;NU1904</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Accepting a specific advisory (FR-007): only when NO patched version
    exists. Suppress by advisory URL, one item per advisory, each with a
    justification comment directly above it:

      <ItemGroup>
        <!- - Accepted YYYY-MM-DD: <why it isn't exploitable here / why there
             is no alternative>. Remove when <package> ships a fix. - ->
        <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx" />
      </ItemGroup>

    Never use NoWarn for NU1901-NU1904. It silences by severity, which would
    hide every future advisory of that severity, not just the accepted one.

    No advisories are currently accepted.
  -->

</Project>
